## TL;DR  

1. **Upgrade to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0** (the version that understands an array of sprite sheets).  
2. **Add your sheet to the style** – either by editing the style JSON or by calling `map.addSprite()` after the style has loaded.  
3. **Reference the icons with the sheet-id prefix** (`"my-icons:restaurant"`).  
4. Verify with `map.hasImage()` / `styleimagemissing` that the image is really loaded.  

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a few “gotchas” that explain why you saw no error and why the icons never appeared.

---

## 1. Why the icons never showed up

| What you did | What MapLibre expected | What actually happened |
|--------------|------------------------|------------------------|
| `sprite: "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap"` (string) | One sprite sheet only. All `icon-image` values are looked up **only** in that sheet. | Your POI icons are not in that sheet, so the lookup fails. |
| `icon-image: "cafe"` (your POI name) | Look for `"cafe"` in the *basemap* sprite JSON. | Not found → the engine silently skips the icon (it fires `styleimagemissing`, but the default console handler only prints a *single* warning per missing id; if the same id is missing on many features you may not notice it). |
| No error in the console | `styleimagemissing` is emitted, but you didn’t listen for it. | You thought “nothing is wrong”. |

The fix is to give the map **more than one sprite sheet** and to tell it **which sheet** to use for each icon.

---

## 2. The modern way: an array of sprite sheets

Since **MapLibre GL JS 3.0** the `sprite` property can be either:

* a **string** (the old single-sheet form), **or**
* an **array of objects** `{ id, url }`.

Each entry is still an *extension-less* base URL; MapLibre will request `url + ".json"` and `url + ".png"` (and the `@2x` variants) automatically.

### 2.1. Edit the style JSON (preferred)

If you control the style file (e.g. you host it yourself or you can edit the JSON before handing it to the map), replace the single string with an array:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    {
      "id": "default",                     // <-- the basemap’s sheet
      "url": "https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap"
    },
    {
      "id": "my-icons",                    // <-- your own sheet
      "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"
    }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "cafes",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "source-layer": "poi",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-icons:cafe"      // <-- note the prefix
      }
    }
    /* … other layers … */
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

* **`id: "default"`** is a special identifier. All images that *don’t* have a prefix are looked up in the sheet whose `id` is `"default"`. This lets you keep the original style unchanged for the basemap icons.
* **Your sheet gets any id you like** (`my-icons` in the example).  
* **Reference an image with `"<sheet-id>:<image-name>"`**. If you forget the prefix, MapLibre will only search the default sheet and the icon will be missing again.
* **All URLs must be absolute and *without* an extension**.  
  *Wrong:* `"https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json"` → MapLibre will request `poi.json.json`.  
  *Correct:* `"https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"`.

### 2.2. Add the sheet at runtime (if you cannot edit the style)

If the style is served from a third-party source that you cannot modify, you can still attach a second sheet **after the style has loaded**:

```js
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add your sprite sheet
  map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');

  // 2️⃣ (Optional) Force a re-evaluation of layers that already exist.
  //    Usually not needed – the map will automatically re-load the
  //    sprite index and any layer that references "my-icons:…" will start
  //    drawing as soon as the PNG/JSON are fetched.
});
```

After `addSprite()` finishes (it returns a `Promise` in v6+, but you can just wait for the `styledata` event if you need to be sure), you can use the same `"my-icons:<name>"` syntax in any layer you add later, **or** you can update existing layers:

```js
// Example: change an existing layer that was using the wrong name
map.setLayoutProperty('cafes', 'icon-image', 'my-icons:cafe');
```

> **Why no error appeared?**  
> When a sprite sheet fails to load, MapLibre emits a generic `error` event (which goes to `console.error` only if nobody listens). When an individual image ID is missing, it emits `styleimagemissing` **once per missing ID**. If you never added a prefix, the map kept looking for `"cafe"` in the default sheet, didn’t find it, and printed a single warning that you may have missed. Adding the second sheet and the prefix makes the lookup succeed, so the warning disappears.

---

## 3. Full working example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – multiple sprite sheets</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // 1️⃣ Two sprite sheets
      sprite: [
        { id: 'default', url: 'https://basemap.provider.com/sprites/basemap' },
        { id: 'my-icons', url: 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi' }
      ],
      sources: {
        // a tiny GeoJSON source with two points
        places: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: {
            type: 'FeatureCollection',
            features: [
              { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] }, properties: { name: 'Cafe A' } },
              { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.43, 37.77] }, properties: { name: 'Park B' } }
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'my-pois',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'places',
          layout: {
            // 2️⃣ Prefix with the sheet id
            'icon-image': 'my-icons:cafe',   // <-- uses my-icons sheet
            'icon-size': 1.2,
            'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
            'text-offset': [0, 1.5],
            'text-anchor': 'top'
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 13
  });

  // Optional: listen for missing-image warnings (helps debugging)
  map.on('styleimagemissing', e => {
    console.warn('Missing image:', e.id);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the two URLs with your real basemap sprite URL and your own POI sprite URL.*  
When you open the page you should see the POI icons drawn from `my-icons` and the basemap icons from `default`.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| POI icons still missing after adding the sheet | **Prefix omitted** (`icon-image: "cafe"` instead of `"my-icons:cafe"`). | Add the `sheet-id:` prefix. |
| `styleimagemissing` fires for `"my-icons:cafe"` | The **sheet URL is wrong** (typo, missing CORS header, or you added `.json`/`.png` extension). | Verify the URL points to the *base* (no extension). Open `https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json` and `.../poi.png` in a browser – they should return 200 with correct MIME (`application/json` and `image/png`). |
| `styleimagemissing` does **not** fire at all, but icons are invisible | The **sprite PNG failed to load** (CORS, 404, 429). The map silently falls back to an empty image. | Open the PNG directly, check CORS (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` or your domain). If you’re using GitHub `raw.githubusercontent.com`, switch to a proper static host (GitHub Pages, Cloudflare R2, Netlify, etc.). |
| `map.addSprite()` throws “Invalid sprite URL … must be absolute” | You passed an object instead of a string, or you included an extension. | Call `map.addSprite('my-icons', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi')`. |
| You’re on MapLibre GL JS 2.x and the array form is rejected | The library version does not support multiple sprite sheets. | **Upgrade** to ≥ 3.0 (the current stable is 3.2 at the time of writing). |
| Icons appear but are all the same size, ignoring `icon-size` | The sprite was built with **SDF** (`sdf: true`) but you’re treating it as a regular bitmap, or vice-versa. | For ordinary PNG icons keep `sdf: false` (default). If you need color-tinting, rebuild the sprite with SDF (or use `addImage` with `sdf: true`). |
| You want to keep the original style untouched **and** you cannot upgrade the library | Use `map.addImage()` for each POI icon instead of a sprite sheet. This is more network-heavy but works on any version. | See the “Runtime images with `addImage`” section of the skill. |

---

## 5. Quick checklist before you ship

1. **MapLibre version** – `>= 3.0`. (`console.log(map.version)` should show `3.x` or higher.)  
2. **Style JSON** – `sprite` is an array with a `default` entry for the basemap and a unique `id` for your sheet.  
3. **Image references** – every `icon-image` that lives in your sheet uses the `id:` prefix.  
4. **CORS** – both sprite PNG and JSON must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or at least your site’s origin).  
5. **No file extensions** – URLs end with the base name only (`.../poi`), not `.json` or `.png`.  
6. **Test locally** – open the dev tools, filter for `styleimagemissing` or `error` events, and confirm `map.hasImage('my-icons:cafe')` returns `true`.  
7. **Cache busting** – if you update the sprite, change the URL (e.g. add a query string `?v=20241026`) so browsers fetch the

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste-ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, rasterised into an `HTMLImageElement`, and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains ~5 000 point features, each with a `category` property that matches one of the SVG file names (e.g. `cafe.svg`, `park.svg`, …).  
* A single **symbol layer** draws the icons.  
* The default collision-detection (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) automatically hides icons that would otherwise pile up when the map is zoomed out.  
* Optional tweaks (`icon-optional: true`, `icon-size`, `icon-text-fit`, etc.) are shown but commented-out – you can enable them if you need a different behaviour.

