## 🎯  What the code does, step-by-step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Map creation** | Loads a basemap style (any style works). | Gives you a canvas to draw on. |
| **`loadSvgImage()`** | Rasterises each SVG into an `Image` element. | `map.addImage()` only accepts raster images; the SVG is turned into a bitmap at its intrinsic size. |
| **`iconNames` list** | Central place to keep the 40 + category identifiers. | Guarantees the IDs you register match the `category` values in the GeoJSON. |
| **`Promise.all`** | Waits for *all* SVGs to finish loading before proceeding. | Guarantees the symbol layer can find every icon immediately after it is added. |
| **`map.addImage(name, img)`** | Registers each rasterised SVG under the same ID as the file name (without extension). | Symbol layers reference icons by this ID (`icon-image`). |
| **`map.addSource('venues', …)`** | Adds the 5 000-point GeoJSON source. | The data the layer will visualise. |
| **`map.addLayer({type:'symbol', …})`** | Draws a point for each feature, using the `category` property to pick the icon. | One layer, no need for 40 separate layers. |
| **Collision handling** (`icon-allow-overlap` default = `false`) | Overlapping icons are automatically hidden based on the map’s collision algorithm. | Prevents the “piling-up” you asked for when zoomed out. |
| **Optional tweaks** (commented) | You can enable `icon-optional`, `icon-size`, `icon-text-fit`, etc., if you later need them. | Gives you a ready-to-use template for future customisation. |

---

## 📦  Things to double-check before you go live

1. **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or at least allow your site). Otherwise `map.addImage()` will throw a security error.
2. **Icon size** – If your SVGs are very large (e.g., 256 × 256 px) they will appear huge on the map. Either:
   * Resize the SVGs to a reasonable pixel size (e.g., 24 × 24 px), **or**
   * Use the `icon-size` layout property to scale them down (`0.5`, `0.8`, …).
3. **Naming consistency** – The `category` property in every GeoJSON feature **must exactly match** one of the IDs you added (`cafe`, `park`, …). A mismatch will fire the `styleimagemissing` event and you’ll see a console warning like `Image "cafe"` could not be loaded.
4. **Performance** – 5 000 points is well within the limits of a single symbol layer. If you ever need > 50 000 points, consider clustering or the “large-data” guide (set `icon-allow-overlap: true` and use `symbol-sort-key` to control which icons survive).
5. **Sprite vs. addImage** – Because you own the icons and they are not part of the base style, `addImage` is the simplest approach. If you later want to bundle them into a sprite sheet (one PNG + JSON request), you can generate a sprite with `spritezero` or `spreet` and switch the style’s `sprite` property instead.

