**Short answer**

No – “`text-anchor` missing **and** `symbol-placement === 'point'`” is far too generic.  
A lot of ordinary point-symbol layers (POI icons, place-name labels, custom markers, etc.) have exactly those two values, so they will be mis-identified as shields.  

A more reliable heuristic is to look at the *icon* side of the layer, because a shield is **always** drawn with a badge image that comes from the sprite sheet, and that badge is paired with a text label that shows the route number. In practice a shield layer has:

| Property | Typical value for a shield | Typical value for a non-shield point layer |
|----------|----------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| `layout['icon-image']` | **present** – usually an expression that resolves to a sprite name like `"{network}_{ref_length}"` or a literal that matches the shield-badge naming convention (`us-interstate_*`, `road_*`, …) | **absent** or a static POI name (`cafe`, `park`, …) |
| `layout['icon-text-fit']` | **set** (`"both"` or `"width"`). The badge is stretchable or at least sized to fit the route number. | **not set** (default `none`). |
| `layout['text-field']` | **references the route reference** – e.g. `"{ref}"` or an expression that pulls the `ref` property. | Usually a place-name field (`"{name}"`, `"{name_en}"`, …) or a constant. |
| `layout['symbol-placement']` | **often a zoom-function / expression** (`{ "base": 1, "stops": [[7,"point"],[8,"line"]] }`) because shields appear as points at low zoom and as line-labels at higher zoom. | Usually a plain string (`"point"`). |
| `layout['text-anchor']` | **usually omitted** (defaults to `"center"`). | May be omitted as well – not a discriminant. |
| `paint['icon-color']` / `icon-halo-*` | **absent** (shields are *not* SDF icons). | May be present for SDF POI icons. |

So the **key** to a shield is **the presence of a badge image (`icon-image`) that follows the shield-naming pattern, together with a `text-field` that shows the route number**. Anything that only has a point placement and no icon-image is *not* a shield.

---

## Why the current heuristic fails

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // flag as shield
}
```

* `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"` when omitted, so **most** symbol layers will have it `=== undefined`.
* `symbol-placement` defaults to `"point"` for point-symbol layers, which includes **every POI, place-name, custom marker, etc.**
* The check does **not** look at the *icon* at all, which is the defining part of a shield.
* It also assumes `symbol-placement` is a plain string, but many shield layers use a **zoom-function object** (or an expression) that resolves to `"point"` at low zoom and `"line"` at higher zoom. In those cases the test will miss the shield entirely.

Result: **false-positives** (plain place-name layers flagged as shields) and **false-negatives** (real shield layers that use a zoom function are skipped).

---

## A more robust heuristic

Below is a compact, production-ready snippet that you can drop into a style-audit script. It works with both the old string-only `sprite` and the newer array-form (`id:image`) because it only inspects the layer definition, not the sprite itself.

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route-shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  // 1️⃣ Must be a symbol layer
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 2️⃣ Must have an icon-image (badge) that follows a shield naming pattern
  const iconImage = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!iconImage) return false; // no badge → not a shield

  // icon-image can be a string or an expression array.
  // We only care about the *resolved* name pattern, so we test the raw value.
  // If it is an expression we conservatively assume it could be a shield.
  const shieldNamePattern = /^(?:[^:]+:)?(?:us|road|default|[^_]+)_\d+$/i;
  const looksLikeShieldName = typeof iconImage === 'string'
    ? shieldNamePattern.test(iconImage)
    : true; // expression → keep checking other clues

  if (!looksLikeShieldName) return false;

  // 3️⃣ Must have a text field that shows the route reference.
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (!textField) return false;
  // Typical values: "{ref}", ["get","ref"], or an expression that contains "ref"
  const refPattern = /ref/i;
  const hasRef = typeof textField === 'string'
    ? refPattern.test(textField)
    : (Array.isArray(textField) && textField.some(part => typeof part === 'string' && refPattern.test(part)));
  if (!hasRef) return false;

  // 4️⃣ Optional but strong signal: icon-text-fit is set (stretchable badge)
  const iconTextFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  if (iconTextFit && iconTextFit !== 'none') return true;

  // 5️⃣ If we got this far, we already have the two defining pieces (badge + ref)
  //    – treat it as a shield even if icon-text-fit is missing.
  return true;
}
```

### How the heuristic works

| Step | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Symbol layer** | Shields are always drawn with a `symbol` layer. |
| **2️⃣ `icon-image` present & matches a shield-badge pattern** | The badge is the only thing that distinguishes a shield from a plain POI. The regex accepts both the default sheet (`road_3`) and prefixed sheets (`my-icons:us-interstate_2`). If `icon-image` is an expression we cannot parse the exact name, so we keep the layer in the candidate set and let later checks decide. |
| **3️⃣ `text-field` contains a reference to `ref`** | Shields always label the route number. Place-name layers usually use `{name}` or `{name_en}`. |
| **4️⃣ `icon-text-fit` set** | Most modern shield sheets make the badge stretchable (`icon-text-fit: "both"`). If this flag is present we can be *certain* it’s a shield. |
| **5️⃣ Fallback** | Some older OSM Bright / OSM Liberty styles use non-stretchable badges (no `icon-text-fit`). In those cases the presence of a badge name and a `ref` text field is enough to call it a shield. |

#### What this *does not* rely on

* **`text-anchor`** – defaults to `"center"` for both shields and place-names, so it’s not discriminating.
* **`symbol-placement`** – shields often use a zoom-function object, which would break a simple string comparison. The heuristic ignores it entirely.
* **`icon-color` / `icon-halo-*`** – shields are *not* SDF icons, but many POI icons are SDF; checking for the absence of `sdf:true` is a weaker signal and not needed once we have the badge-+-ref combo.

---

## How to adapt the script for the new **multiple-sprite** form

If your style uses the array form (`sprite: [{id:"default",url:…},{id:"my-icons",url:…}]`) the `icon-image` value will be prefixed with the sheet id, e.g. `"my-icons:us-interstate_2"`. The regex above already allows an optional `[^:]+:` prefix, so you don’t need any extra handling.

If you want to be extra-strict and verify that the referenced sheet actually contains the badge, you can:

1. Load each sprite JSON (e.g. `fetch(url + ".json")`).
2. Build a `Set` of all image IDs (`default:image`, `my-icons:image`).
3. In `isShieldLayer`, after step 2, check `set.has(iconImage)` (or the prefixed version).

That extra step is useful for a **style-audit** that also validates that the sprite URLs are reachable.

---

## Quick checklist for a manual audit

When you glance at a layer definition, ask yourself:

| Question | Expected for a shield | Expected for a plain point layer |
|----------|----------------------|----------------------------------|
| Does the layer have `type: "symbol"`? | Yes | Yes (but also many POIs) |
| Is there an `icon-image`? | **Yes**, and the name looks like `*_#` (e.g. `road_3`, `us-interstate_2`, `my-icons:road_4`) | May be **yes** (e.g. `cafe`, `park`) **or no** (pure text label) |
| Does `text-field` contain `{ref}` or an expression that pulls `ref`? | **Yes** | Usually `{name}` or `{name_en}` |
| Is `icon-text-fit` set to `"both"` / `"width"`? | Often **yes** (stretchable badge) | Usually **absent** |
| Is `symbol-placement` a plain `"point"`? | May be a **zoom-function** object (point → line) – *ignore* this field for shields. | Usually a plain `"point"` – but that alone is not enough. |
| Does the sprite sheet actually contain the badge image? | **Yes** (verify with `listImages()` or by inspecting the sprite JSON) | May be any POI icon, not a shield badge. |

If the answer to the first three rows is “yes”, you have a shield. The fourth row (icon-text-fit) is a strong confirm-or-reject, but not required for older styles.

---

## TL;DR – What to change in your script

Replace the current test:

```js
if (layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
    layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point') {
    // flag as shield
}
```

with something like:

```js
if (isShieldLayer(layer)) {
    // flag as shield
}
```

where `isShieldLayer` implements the logic above (badge name + ref text field, optionally `icon-text-fit`). This will:

* **Stop flagging ordinary place-name layers** (they lack a badge image and a `{ref}` text field).
* **Catch real shield layers** even when they use a zoom-function for `symbol-placement` or when the badge is stretchable.
* **Work with both single-sprite and multi-sprite styles** without extra code.

Feel free to tweak the regex (`shieldNamePattern`) if your own sprite sheet uses a different naming convention (e.g. `shield_us_i_2`, `highwayBadge_3`, etc.). The core idea is: **look for a badge image and a route-ref text field**, not for the absence of `text-anchor`.

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste** example that shows how to

1. **Create a MapLibre GL JS map**  
2. **Add a single draggable pin (Marker) at a known coordinate**  
3. **Attach a Popup that opens when the user clicks the pin**  
4. (Optional) keep the popup in sync with the pin’s new location after a drag.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Draggable pin with popup – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style you like
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],             // lon, lat – San Francisco
    zoom: 12
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Wait until the style is fully loaded before adding UI
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Create a Popup (it can be empty now)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML('<strong>Drag me!</strong>');

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Create a draggable Marker at the same coordinate
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <-- makes the pin draggable
      color: '#ff0000'          // optional – red pin (default is blue)
    })
      .setLngLat([-122.42, 37.77])   // initial position
      .setPopup(popup)               // attach the popup we created
      .addTo(map);                   // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  Open the popup when the user clicks the pin
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The popup is automatically opened on a click because we called
    // `marker.setPopup(popup)`.  If you want to control it yourself you can
    // listen for the marker’s `click` event:
    // marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => popup.toggle());

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣  (Optional) Keep the popup’s content in sync after a drag
    // -------------------------------------------------
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      // Update the popup text – you could also fire an API request here.
      popup.setHTML(
        `<strong>New position:</strong><br/>` +
        `lon: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}<br/>` +
        `lat: ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`
      );
      // If the popup is open, move it to the new location.
      if (popup.isOpen()) popup.setLngLat(lngLat);
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## How It Works – Step-by-step

| Step | What you do | Why it matters |
|------|-------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | You need a map instance before you can add any UI. |
| **2️⃣ Wait for `load`** | `map.on('load', …)` | The style (including sprites) must be ready; otherwise the marker could be added before the map can render it. |
| **3️⃣ Create a `Popup`** | `new maplibregl.Popup()` | The popup is a separate object that can be attached to a marker, opened, closed, or moved later. |
| **4️⃣ Create a draggable `Marker`** | `new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable:true })` | `draggable:true` makes the pin movable with the mouse/touch. |
| **5️⃣ Attach the popup** | `marker.setPopup(popup)` | When a marker has a popup, MapLibre automatically opens it on a click (or you can call `popup.toggle()` yourself). |
| **6️⃣ Add the marker to the map** | `.addTo(map)` | Renders the pin on the canvas. |
| **7️⃣ (Optional) Listen for `dragend`** | `marker.on('dragend', …)` | Lets you react to the new coordinate – e.g., update the popup text, store the location, or send it to a server. |

### A few practical tips

| Situation | Recommended tweak |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **Custom pin graphics** | Use `new maplibregl.Marker({ element: myHtmlElement })` or `new maplibregl.Marker({ color: '#ff6600' })`. |
| **Multiple pins** | Keep an array of markers; each can have its own popup. |
| **Persist the location** | In the `dragend` handler, `fetch('/save', {method:'POST', body: JSON.stringify(lngLat)})`. |
| **Show the popup on map load** | Call `popup.addTo(map)` *or* `marker.togglePopup()` after `addTo(map)`. |
| **Prevent the map from panning while dragging** | The built-in draggable marker already disables map drag while you hold the pin. |
| **Styling the popup** | The popup’s HTML can contain any markup; you can also add a CSS class via `popup.addClassName('my-popup')`. |

---

### TL;DR Code Snippet (the essentials)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({ offset: 25 })
                .setHTML('Click me!');

  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
                .setLngLat([lon, lat])
                .setPopup(popup)   // click → popup
                .addTo(map);

  marker.on('dragend', () => {
    const pos = marker.getLngLat();
    popup.setHTML(`lon: ${pos.lng.toFixed(5)}<br>lat: ${pos.lat.toFixed(5)}`);
    if (popup.isOpen()) popup.setLngLat(pos);
  });
});
```

That’s all you need to place a **draggable pin** on a MapLibre GL JS map and show a **popup** when the user clicks it. Happy mapping!
